T(15,'1964-11-02','M. I. Vishik','P000034-P000037','Fredholm index, nonlinear equations, and families',r'P000035 moves from Fredholm perturbation invariance to nonlinear equations and parameter families. The homotopy claim is meaningful only after specifying the operator space. Catalogue envelopes overlap adjacent records; the item locators identify the actual passage.')
I(15,1,'P000035','theorem','Stability of the Fredholm index',r'The notes set \(k=\dim\ker T\), \(\ell=\dim\operatorname{coker}K\), \(\ae=k-\ell\), unchanged by small or completely continuous perturbations.',r'For a bounded Fredholm \(T:X\to Y\) between Banach spaces, the index is locally constant in operator norm and unchanged by addition of a compact operator.','E',r'The background is explicitly recalled by \citet[p.~861, Theorem~1.1]{smale1965sard}; the elliptic setting already appears in \citet[p.~423]{atiyahsinger1963index}.','both invariance assertions hold with Fredholmness, closed range, and a fixed topology.', ['smale1965sard','atiyahsinger1963index'],reconstruction=r'Banach spaces, boundedness and norm topology are supplied; the isolated K is normalized to T.')
I(15,2,'P000035','claim','What the integer index classifies',r'Vishik considers index the only homotopy invariant.',r'For infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert \(H\), the path components of the norm-topological \(\operatorname{Fred}(H)\) are classified by the integer index. For compact Hausdorff \(X\), families instead satisfy \([X,\operatorname{Fred}(H)]\cong K^0(X)\).','B',r'\citet[pp.~129--142]{janich1965fredholm}, published April 1965, gives the precise family formulation. The notebook does not specify the intended ambient space.','the component statement is true in the full Fredholm space; it does not classify restricted elliptic symbols or all families.', ['janich1965fredholm'],reconstruction=r'Ambient Hilbert space and topology supplied.',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',confidence='medium')
I(15,3,'P000035','question','Index for nonlinear elliptic maps',r'What are kernel, cokernel and index for a nonlinear equation? Linearize at every point.',r'A \(C^1\) map \(F:M\to N\) of Banach manifolds is Fredholm if every \(DF_x\) is Fredholm. Its derivative index is locally constant, hence constant on connected \(M\). This does not define vector-space kernels or cokernels for \(F\) itself.','B',r'\citet[Theorem~1.3 and Section~2]{smale1965sard} gives the Fredholm-map framework and elliptic applications. Received 2 November 1964, the day of this seminar, and published October 1965; a causal connection is not established.','Fredholm-map theory supplies the precise answer in this framework.', ['smale1965sard'],problem='GP-1964-W01',reconstruction=r'The Banach-manifold and Fredholm-derivative assumptions are supplied.')
I(15,4,'P000035','question','Stable invariant of a family',r'For \(T_x\), \(x\in X\), an invariant of the whole family is sought.',r'For norm-continuous \(T:X\to\operatorname{Fred}(H)\), with compact Hausdorff \(X\), choose finite-dimensional \(V\) with \(T_xH+V=H\). Then \(E_x=T_x^{-1}V\) is a bundle and \([E]-[X\times V]\in K^0(X)\) is independent of \(V\).','B',r'\citet[pp.~129--142]{janich1965fredholm} identifies the Fredholm classifying space in 1965, giving the family invariant. No transmission from this seminar is claimed.','the index bundle answers the stable question; its rank is the numerical index.', ['janich1965fredholm'],verification='publisher metadata checked',problem='GP-1964-W02',reconstruction=r'Compactness, continuity and stabilization are supplied.')
C('P000035','Fredholm definition','coker K','coker T','The scan introduces no second operator K; this is a notebook notation slip.')

T(16,'1964-11-02','A. Ya. Povzner','P000035-P000038','Statistical-mechanics programme and particle-energy fragment',r'P000037 proposes Ruelle, Yang--Lee and Kac readings, then sketches a pair-potential minimization claim. The handwritten given-name attribution conflicts with the catalogue; neither a uniform confinement theorem nor its assumptions can be recovered.')
I(16,1,'P000037','remark','Statistical-mechanics reading programme',r'Two or three Ruelle works, one Yang--Lee work, and Kac models are proposed.','A reading programme in rigorous equilibrium statistical mechanics, rather than a theorem.','E',r'Ruelle author metadata confirms \citet[pp.~183--197]{ruelle1963classical} and adjacent 1963--64 papers as plausible reading. The unspecified list cannot be uniquely reconstructed.','the subject is identifiable but the exact proposed bibliography is not.', ['ruelle1963classical'],literal='correct',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium')
F(16,2,'P000037','claim','Confinement of pair-potential minimizers',r'A repulsive-core/attractive-well graph accompanies a double interaction sum; a minimizing configuration is said to lie in a sphere of radius \(C\). Nearby powers of \(N\) are unclear.',r'potential hypotheses, treatment of self-interactions, existence, dimension and dependence of \(C\) on particle number are absent.','Povzner/Ruelle, ground-state, confinement and pair-potential searches did not locate this exact statement. Thermodynamic stability alone does not supply it.')
C('P000037','Povzner heading','Povzner. Naum Yakovlevich','Retain attribution conflict with catalogue A. Ya. Povzner','The scan contains the unexpected given name; no supported substitution is available.',typ='provenance')

T(17,'1964-11-16','I. M. Gelfand','P000036-P000038','Invariant-subspace announcement and Schur positivity',r'The enthusiastic invariant-subspace announcement on P000037 is followed by Schur positivity. The first records a subsequently retracted claim, not a solution.')
I(17,1,'P000037','claim','Retracted invariant-subspace announcement',r'\ru{Решено!!!} accompanies a reference to a 1964 Bulletin AMS de Branges paper and a second author, with Livshits mentioned.',r'The intended question is whether every bounded operator on a separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space has a closed invariant subspace other than \(0,H\). The de Branges--Rovnyak affirmative announcement was retracted.','C','The 1964 announcement and 1965 retraction are audited with record~50. This fragment identifies the announcement rather than a valid proof.','the general problem remains distinct from its solved special cases and later unverified claims; the present record supplies no solution.',literal='false as written',verification='indirect',problem='GP-1964-02',worth='Worth investigating in precise special classes; the unrestricted problem exceeds a bounded development.',reconstruction=r'Boundedness, complex scalars, separability and closed nontrivial subspace are supplied.')
I(17,2,'P000037','theorem','Schur positivity of generalized determinants',r'Generalized determinants of a positive matrix are positive, by Schur.',r'For Hermitian positive semidefinite \(A=(a_{ij})\) and an irreducible character \(\chi\) of \(S_n\), the immanant \(\sum_{\sigma}\chi(\sigma)\prod_i a_{i,\sigma(i)}\) is nonnegative; it is positive for positive definite \(A\).','E',r'The antecedent is \citet[pp.~184--207]{schur1918formen}, published June 1918. The particular generalized determinant is not defined in the note.','the character-immanant reading is established; entrywise positivity alone is insufficient.', ['schur1918formen'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'Hermitian positivity, irreducible characters and nonnegative in the semidefinite case are supplied.',confidence='medium')

T(18,'1964-11-16','V. I. Arnold','P000036-P000039','Semiclassical transport and caustic phase',r'The action/Jacobian description is introduced before the caustic phase problem. This foreshadows the 1967 characteristic-class paper; fuller formulas appear on 23 November.')
I(18,1,'P000037-P000038','method','WKB transport before a caustic',r'The wave has an action phase and square-root Jacobian amplitude.',r'For \(ih\psi_t=(-h^2\Delta/2+V)\psi\), \(\psi=ae^{iS/h}\) gives \(S_t+|\nabla S|^2/2+V=0\) and \(a_t+\nabla S\cdot\nabla a+(\Delta S)a/2=0\) at leading orders. The amplitude is a transported half-density.','E',r'This classical local construction is the starting point of \citet[Section~1]{arnold1967characteristic}.','the formula applies where the characteristic projection is nonsingular.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'A consistent normalization and formal leading-order interpretation are supplied.')
I(18,2,'P000038','claim','Characteristic class of caustic phase',r'Caustic crossings carry a topological phase correction.',r'A smooth Lagrangian Gauss map pulls back the integral degree-one Maslov class of the Lagrangian Grassmannian. In a standard convention its loop phase is \(\exp(-i\pi\mu/2)\).','A',r'\citet[Sections~1--3]{arnold1967characteristic}, received 28 September 1966 and published 1967, gives the characteristic class. These notes witness November 1964 oral circulation.','the Maslov-class formulation is established, with convention-dependent phase sign.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'The Grassmannian construction is the later precise identification.')

T(19,'1964-11-16','Yu. V. Linnik','P000037-P000041','Tests, nuisance parameters and analytic ideals',r'The record passes from sufficiency and randomized tests to similarity, nuisance variances, Bayes-weighted cotests and analytic ideal methods. The closely matching paper was submitted in December 1964.')
I(19,1,'P000038','definition','Experiments and sufficient statistics',r'Distribution families and statistics are introduced, with a Gaussian sample-mean example.',r'In a dominated experiment \(\{P_\theta\}\), sufficiency means that the conditional law of the sample given a statistic is independent of \(\theta\). For independent \(N(\theta,\sigma^2)\) observations with known positive variance, the sample mean is sufficient.','E',r'These are classical preliminaries used in \citet[p.~520]{linnik1965cotest}.','the Gaussian example needs known variance; the mean alone is insufficient when variance is also unknown.', ['linnik1965cotest'],match='partial',reconstruction=r'Domination, independence and known variance are supplied.')
I(19,2,'P000038-P000039','definition','Randomized and similar tests',r'Power is maximized subject to a type-I restriction; constant null rejection probability is described using an invariance term.',r'A randomized test is measurable \(\phi:\Omega\to[0,1]\). Level \(\alpha\) requires \(\sup_{\Theta_0}E_\theta\phi\leq\alpha\); similarity requires equality for every null parameter. Group invariance is a different condition.','E',r'\citet[p.~520]{linnik1965cotest} explicitly defines similarity, clarifying the notebook vocabulary.','level, similarity and group invariance must be distinguished.', ['linnik1965cotest'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The terminology is corrected, not an invariance theorem asserted.')
I(19,3,'P000039','problem','Normal means with unknown unequal variances',r'Comparison of two Gaussian means is the nuisance-parameter example.',r'Given independent samples \(X_i\sim N(\mu_1,\sigma_1^2)\), \(Y_j\sim N(\mu_2,\sigma_2^2)\), with unknown positive variances, test \(\mu_1=\mu_2\). Optimality additionally requires a level, sidedness, admissible test class and alternative power criterion.','E',r'This is the classical Behrens--Fisher setting. \citet[pp.~520--522]{linnik1965cotest} supplies a general weighted optimization framework, not an unrestricted uniformly most powerful solution.','the model is definite, but no single remaining optimality conjecture is specified.', ['linnik1965cotest'],match='thematic only',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'The model is normalized; no universal optimum is inferred.')
I(19,4,'P000039-P000040','method','Bayes-weighted cotest optimization',r'An alternative measure and a cotest ideal encode the testing constraints.',r'For a similar test set \(l=\phi-\alpha\). Then \(E_\theta l=0\) on \(\Theta_0\), \(-\alpha\leq l\leq1-\alpha\). A fixed alternative measure \(B\) gives objective \(\int E_\theta l\,dB(\theta)\). Under analytic-transform hypotheses, null-zero constraints become ideal membership.','A',r'\citet[pp.~520--522]{linnik1965cotest} gives this construction. Received 7 December 1964, published 1965, following the seminar by three weeks.','the framework is identifiable; its conclusions depend on the transform range and weighting.', ['linnik1965cotest'],reconstruction=r'The bounds and fixed alternative weighting make the problem precise.')
I(19,5,'P000040','method','Analytic ideals in statistical testing',r'Cartan, Oka and division organize cotest constraints.',r'A coherent null-constraint ideal on a Stein parameter domain admits global patching of local division by Cartan B. This does not give boundary-continuous division or guarantee that every holomorphic function is a statistical transform.','A',r'\citet[p.~521]{linnik1965cotest} applies analytic-ideal generation under specified conditions on the null set; the application is new, the sheaf machinery older.','the analytic reduction is established with those hypotheses.', ['linnik1965cotest'],match='partial',reconstruction=r'The Stein/coherent formulation explains the method; not all these hypotheses appear in the note.')
F(19,6,'P000040','question','Unspecified primary ideals on a ball',r'Primary ideals for functions on a ball are mentioned.',r'the algebra could be holomorphic, bounded holomorphic or boundary-continuous, and no support or ideal is specified.','Linnik/Palamodov/analytic-division searches identify the surrounding programme, but no unique problem; Gorin next states a precise maximal-ideal question.')
C('P000039','test terminology','invariant and similar tests conflated','similar means constant null rejection probability; invariant requires a specified group','The scan retains the terminology; Linnik 1965 p520 explicitly defines similarity.')

T(20,'1964-11-16','E. A. Gorin','P000039-P000042','Gleason problem for the ball algebra',r'The precise question is coordinate generation of the maximal ideal at the origin, preserving continuity on the closed ball. Comparisons with the disk, polydisc and unrestricted holomorphic division isolate the boundary issue. The ball case is solved.')
I(20,1,'P000041','question','Coordinate generators of the ball-algebra ideal',r'If \(f(0)=0\), can \(f=z_1g_1+z_2g_2\), with all functions holomorphic in the ball and continuous on its closure?',r'For \(A(B^n)=\mathcal O(B^n)\cap C(\overline B^n)\) and \(a\in B^n\), the ideal \(\{f:f(a)=0\}\) is generated by \(z_j-a_j\). The source asks \(n=2,a=0\).','B',r'\citet[pp.~595--596, reference Hen]{backlundfallstrom1998gleason} records Leibenzon’s affirmative solution and Henkin’s 1971 publication. \citet[pp.~125--132]{gleason1964ideals} is the contemporary antecedent. Initial circulation of Leibenzon’s proof was not verified.','the exact ball question is solved; later counterexamples concern other domains.', ['backlundfallstrom1998gleason','gleason1964ideals'],problem='GP-1964-W03',verification='indirect',reconstruction=r'The general \(n,a\) version records the known extension.')
I(20,2,'P000041','construction','Disk and polydisc division',r'The one-variable case is immediate and the product domain is easier.',r'For \(f\in A(\mathbb D)\), \(f(0)=0\), division by \(z\) has a removable singularity and remains boundary-continuous. On \(\mathbb D^n\), successive coordinate telescoping and division produce \(f=\sum z_jg_j\) for \(f(0)=0\).','E',r'The one-variable assertion is also recalled by \citet[p.~596]{backlundfallstrom1998gleason}; the telescoping explanation is elementary reconstruction.','the division holds without assuming uniform boundary convergence of Taylor series.', ['backlundfallstrom1998gleason'],reconstruction=r'The telescoping proof is supplied, not a boundary power-series argument.',match='partial')
I(20,3,'P000041','remark','Division without boundary restrictions',r'The difficulty disappears when boundary continuity is dropped.',r'For Stein \(\Omega\subset\mathbb C^n\) and \(a\in\Omega\), the coherent ideal sheaf of \(a\) is generated by \(z_j-a_j\). Cartan B applied to its relation sheaf yields \(f=\sum(z_j-a_j)g_j\), \(g_j\in\mathcal O(\Omega)\), when \(f(a)=0\).','E',r'The holomorphic-versus-Banach-algebra distinction is explicit in \citet[pp.~595--596]{backlundfallstrom1998gleason}.','the holomorphic statement gives no boundary continuity estimate.', ['backlundfallstrom1998gleason'],reconstruction=r'The sheaf proof and Stein hypothesis are editorial.',match='partial',verification='indirect')

T(21,'1964-11-23','V. I. Arnold','P000040-P000046','WKB geometry, caustics and Morse index',r'Action and Jacobian transport are developed through caustics, then related to optical phase and the Morse index. The notebook has inconsistent Schrödinger normalizations; modern formulas are explicitly reconstructed.')
I(21,1,'P000042-P000043','method','Hamilton--Jacobi equation',r'An exponential substitution relates Schrödinger evolution to classical trajectories.',r'For \(ih\psi_t=(-h^2\Delta/2+V)\psi\), \(\psi=e^{iS/h}(a_0+ha_1+\cdots)\) yields \(S_t+H(x,\nabla S)=0\), \(H=|p|^2/2+V\), formally wherever \(S\) is smooth.','E',r'This local background is used in \citet[Section~1]{arnold1967characteristic}.','the formal relation is standard; the notebook coefficients cannot all hold simultaneously.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Signs, kinetic factor and the formal interpretation are normalized.')
I(21,2,'P000042-P000043','construction','Action and half-density',r'The amplitude contains a square-root trajectory Jacobian and the phase \(\int p\,dx\).',r'For nonsingular \(x=X_t(y)\), \(a_0(t,X_t(y))=a_0(0,y)|\det D_yX_t|^{-1/2}\). The action evolves by \(S(t,X_t(y))=S(0,y)+\int_0^t(p\cdot\dot x-H)\,ds\).','E',r'The geometric setting is \citet[Section~1]{arnold1967characteristic}; these consistent formulas are an editorial reconstruction.','the formula requires a nonsingular projection; abbreviated spatial action needs a fixed-energy or time convention.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'Absolute Jacobian and \(-Hdt\) are supplied.')
I(21,3,'P000044','claim','Maslov correction at caustics',r'A branch sum is corrected by a phase involving \(\pi n_j/2\), associated with Maslov.',r'For regular Lagrangian propagation, oscillatory-integral chart transitions yield Maslov phase factors; the integral Maslov class governs loop phase, conventionally \(e^{-i\pi\mu/2}\).','A',r'\citet[Sections~1--3]{arnold1967characteristic} gives the topological formulation, received September 1966 and published 1967, after these lectures.','the phase class is established; uniform local approximation needs a caustic normal form.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'The phase sign is conventional; the damaged formula does not determine it.')
I(21,4,'P000044','remark','Optical quarter-cycle phase',r'A ray tangent to an envelope acquires a phase jump \(\pi/2\).',r'At a generic fold caustic, an Airy model matches oscillatory branches with a relative quarter-cycle phase under standard conventions. Degenerate caustics require different models.','E',r'This optical motivation precedes the seminar and informs \citet[Section~1]{arnold1967characteristic}.','the fold qualification is necessary.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'Genericity and phase convention supplied.',match='partial')
I(21,5,'P000044','theorem','Morse index and conjugate points',r'The phase index is related to negative squares of the second variation and conjugate points.',r'For a regular fixed-endpoint mechanical extremal with positive kinetic form and nonconjugate endpoints, the Morse index equals the count of interior conjugate points with multiplicities.','E',r'The classical variational theorem is background to \citet[Sections~1--3]{arnold1967characteristic}.','the relation holds in that variational setting; endpoint nullity requires an extra convention.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],reconstruction=r'Regularity, endpoints and multiplicity conventions supplied.',match='partial',verification='indirect')
C('P000042','Schrödinger and action formulas','i/h psi_t = Delta psi - U psi/h^2; integral p dx','Consistent modern normalization ih psi_t=-h^2 Delta psi/2+U psi; action integral (p dx-Hdt)','The coefficients and shorthand are faithfully visible in the scan; the emendation is mathematical.')

T(22,'1964-11-23','I. M. Gelfand','P000044-P000047','Phase volume and the fermionic proof of Sturm oscillation',r'Berezin’s eigenvalue-counting remark precedes Gelfand’s one-dimensional zeros and determinant argument. The latter uses the fermionic ground state; a full implementation appeared in 2018/2020. The notebook’s sign and indexing require correction.')
I(22,1,'P000045','claim','Phase-volume eigenvalue asymptotics',r'Berezin relates a region under the classical Hamiltonian to quantum eigenvalue counting; the written threshold mixes energy and index.',r'For smooth confining \(P_h=-h^2\Delta/2+V\) on \(\mathbb R^d\), with compact regular sublevel at \(E\), \(N_h(E)\sim(2\pi h)^{-d}\operatorname{vol}\{(x,p):|p|^2/2+V(x)<E\}\) as \(h\to0\).','E',r'This is the classical phase-volume law, thematically adjoining the semiclassical geometry of \citet[Section~1]{arnold1967characteristic}; no exact publication of Berezin’s compressed wording is identified.','the reconstructed Weyl law is established under those hypotheses.', ['arnold1967characteristic'],literal='false as written',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',speaker='F. A. Berezin',reconstruction=r'Limit, normalization, dimension and confinement are supplied.')
I(22,2,'P000045','theorem','Sturm zeros and interlacing',r'Dirichlet eigenfunctions on \([0,\pi]\) are discussed; the free eigenvalues have a negative sign in the note.',r'For real continuous \(q\), the Dirichlet operator \(L=-d^2/dx^2+q\) has simple ordered eigenvalues. Its \(j\)-th eigenfunction has \(j-1\) interior zeros interlacing the next. For \(q=0\), \(\lambda_j=j^2\).','E',r'\citet[historical discussion and Sturm theorem]{berardhelffer2020sturm} revisits the 1836 result; preprint 2017, publication 2020.','this is classical; the notebook sign is erroneous for its displayed operator.', ['berardhelffer2020sturm'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Indexing starts at 1 throughout.')
I(22,3,'P000045','theorem','Zeros of the first eigenfunction span',r'A linear combination of the first eigenfunctions has a bounded number of zeros; indexing changes within the note.',r'For a regular real Dirichlet Sturm--Liouville problem with smooth potential, a nonzero combination of the first \(m\) eigenfunctions has at most \(m-1\) interior zeros, counted with multiplicity.','E',r'\citet[main theorem and historical discussion]{berardhelffer2020sturm} identifies Sturm’s 1836 theorem; the preprint circulated June 2017 before 2020 publication.','the bound is established; the seminar’s distinctive feature is its proof strategy.', ['berardhelffer2020sturm'],reconstruction=r'The indexing and interior multiplicity convention resolve the ambiguity.')
I(22,4,'P000045','method','Gelfand’s Slater-determinant proof strategy',r'A determinant of eigenfunction data is related to a many-particle problem; the displayed entries mix values and derivatives.',r'\(\det[u_j(x_i)]_{i,j=1}^m\) is an antisymmetric eigenfunction of \(\sum_i L_{x_i}\). On \(x_1<\cdots<x_m\), it is a ground state of fixed sign, so the evaluation determinant is nonzero for distinct interior points. Multiplicity of zeros requires an additional argument.','B',r'\citet[Sections~3--6]{berardhelffer2020gelfand} explicitly implements Gelfand’s strategy: first preprint 11 July 2018, journal 2020.','the strategy has a rigorous published realization; the notebook determinant alone is incomplete.', ['berardhelffer2020gelfand'],reconstruction=r'Ordered chamber, ground-state argument and the multiplicity distinction are supplied.')
C('P000045','free Dirichlet spectrum','lambda=-n^2','lambda_n=n^2 for the displayed -y-double-prime operator','The scan faithfully has both the operator sign and negative eigenvalue.')
C('P000045','Morse and zero indexing','first eigenfunction has Morse index 1; n zeros','With indexing from 1, index of L-lambda_j is j-1 and the first m span has at most m-1 interior zeros','The notebook mixes zero-based and one-based labels; modern conventions stated explicitly.',typ='ambiguity')
