T(24,'1965-02-08','I. I. Pyateski-Shapiro','P000045-P000048','Congruence subgroups and elementary matrices',r'The record contrasts the congruence-subgroup property in ranks at least two with the modular group. The main integer-matrix result was already available in 1964. The blanket assertion about rank-one power quotients needs a low-level exception and an orbifold qualification.')
I(24,1,'P000046-P000047','theorem','Congruence property for integer special linear groups',r'For \(n\geq3\), every finite-index subgroup of \(SL(n,\mathbb Z)\) contains a congruence subgroup; \(n=2\) differs.',r'For \(n\geq3\) and finite-index \(\Gamma\leq SL_n(\mathbb Z)\), some \(m\geq1\) satisfies \(\ker(SL_n(\mathbb Z)\to SL_n(\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z))\subset\Gamma\). For \(n=2\), finite-index noncongruence subgroups exist.','E',r'\citet[Sections~1.2--1.3, Theorem~1 and historical remark]{serre1968congruence} attributes the rational-integer case to Bass--Lazard--Serre and Mennicke in 1964; the Bourbaki lecture was June 1967, printed 1968.','the stated integer-matrix theorem is settled, not a February 1965 open question.', ['serre1968congruence'],reconstruction=r'The subgroup need not be normal; passage to its finite-index core is allowed.')
I(24,2,'P000047','method','Reduction to powers of elementary transvections',r'The normal subgroup generated by powers of elementary matrices is used to reduce the finite-index question.',r'Given a finite-index subgroup, its normal core contains \(e_{ij}(m)\) for some common positive \(m\), since each elementary generator has finite-order image in the finite quotient. For \(n\geq3\), the congruence theorem then controls the normal elementary subgroup.','E',r'This is the elementary-matrix route in the congruence programme summarized by \citet[Sections~2--3]{serre1968congruence}.','the finite-quotient reduction is valid; identifying elementary and principal congruence subgroups at the same level is not automatic.', ['serre1968congruence'],reconstruction=r'Normal core and common multiple of finite orders are supplied.',match='partial')
I(24,3,'P000047','claim','Rank-one power quotients and triangle groups',r'For \(n=2\), powers of generators are said to give infinite index, with a quotient interpreted as a surface fundamental group.',r'In \(PSL_2(\mathbb Z)=\langle s,t:s^2=(st)^3=1\rangle\), adding \(t^m=1\) gives the triangle group \(\Delta(2,3,m)\). For \(2\leq m\leq5\) it is finite, for \(m=6\) Euclidean, and for \(m\geq7\) hyperbolic. It is an orbifold group, not generally a torsion-free surface group.','E',r'The modular-group contrast is classical; \citet[Section~1.2(b)]{serre1968congruence} confirms the failure of the rank-one congruence property. The explicit presentation and low-level check here are editorial algebra.','the unqualified infinite-index and surface-group assertions are false; a torsion-free finite-index subgroup restores the surface interpretation in the hyperbolic case.', ['serre1968congruence'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Passage from SL to PSL and the triangle-group presentation are essential.')
F(24,4,'P000047','remark','Unspecified elementary congruence transformation',r'An \(a\equiv b\pmod N\) statement is linked to elementary transformations.',r'the entries, matrix operation, modulus conventions and desired normal form are not defined.','Congruence-subgroup sources explain the surrounding method but do not identify this isolated transformation.')
C('P000047','rank-one quotient','infinite index for all N; fundamental group of a surface','Triangle quotient Delta(2,3,N); finite for N=2,3,4,5 and generally an orbifold group','The scan gives the unqualified assertion. The modular presentation exhibits the missing small-level and torsion qualifications.')

T(25,'1965-02-08','I. M. Gelfand','P000046-P000049','Spin-flavour groups and relativistic symmetry',r'An SU(6) discussion is juxtaposed with the Hermitian-matrix model of Minkowski space and proposed larger symmetry groups. The matrix display needs a semidirect-product correction. Coleman--Mandula later answered a restricted precise version, not every unification programme.')
I(25,1,'P000047','construction','SU(6) spin-flavour symmetry',r'Spin SU(2) and internal SU(3) are combined in SU(6).',r'The tensor product on \(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3\) embeds \(SU(2)\times SU(3)\) into \(SU(6)\). This organizes a nonrelativistic approximate spin-flavour model; it is not by itself a Lorentz-covariant exact scattering symmetry.','E',r'\citet[pp.~173--175]{gurseyradicati1964spin} appeared 3 August 1964; \citet[pp.~B1756--B1760]{sakita1964supermultiplets} was received 7 August and published 21 December 1964. Both precede this February record.','the historical SU(6) construction is identified; exact relativistic symmetry needs additional assumptions.', ['gurseyradicati1964spin','sakita1964supermultiplets'],verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The approximation status and tensor-product interpretation are supplied.')
I(25,2,'P000047-P000048','construction','Hermitian matrices and the Poincaré group',r'Lorentz transformations act on Hermitian matrices; a block matrix with diagonal \(A,(A^*)^{-1}\) and Hermitian upper-right block is written.',r'For \(A\in SL_2(\mathbb C)\), \(X=X^*\), the map \(X\mapsto AXA^*\) preserves \(\det X=x_0^2-|\mathbf x|^2\). The covering Poincaré group has law \((A,X)(B,Y)=(AB,X+AYA^*)\), represented by \(\begin{pmatrix}A&X(A^*)^{-1}\\0&(A^*)^{-1}\end{pmatrix}\).','E',r'This is the standard spacetime group underlying \citet[p.~1251]{colemanmandula1967symmetries}; the block-law repair is direct matrix algebra.','the corrected matrices are closed under multiplication. An arbitrary Hermitian upper-right block in the notebook parametrization is not closed.', ['colemanmandula1967symmetries'],literal='false as written',match='thematic only',reconstruction=r'The semidirect product and corrected block are editorial. Setting translations to zero gives the Lorentz cover; spatial rotations require \(A\in SU(2)\).')
I(25,3,'P000048','question','Combining spacetime and internal symmetries',r'A larger symmetry containing relativistic and internal transformations is sought.',r'For a nontrivial analytic scattering matrix in four-dimensional relativistic theory, suppose a connected ordinary Lie symmetry contains the Poincaré group, acts tensorially on multiparticle states, has only finitely many particle types below each mass bound, and satisfies the regularity and nontrivial-scattering hypotheses of Coleman--Mandula. Then it is locally a direct product of Poincaré and internal symmetries.','B',r'\citet[p.~1251, theorem and hypotheses~1--5]{colemanmandula1967symmetries}, received March and published July 1967, gives this restricted obstruction. The original broad programme is not equivalent to the theorem’s hypotheses.','the ordinary-Lie scattering version is settled; graded symmetries and violations of the hypotheses lie outside that conclusion.', ['colemanmandula1967symmetries'],match='partial',reconstruction=r'The precise scattering question is a later formulation; no blanket impossibility theorem is attributed to the notebook.')
F(25,4,'P000048','remark','108- and 144-parameter proposals',r'Groups with 108 and 144 parameters are proposed in the discussion.',r'no Lie brackets, real form, faithful action or particle interpretation identifies the groups uniquely.','SU(6), relativistic supermultiplet and Coleman--Mandula searches contextualize the numbers but do not establish a unique match.')
C('P000048','block Poincaré display','upper-right H Hermitian independently of A','upper-right X(A*)^{-1}, X Hermitian, or use pairs (A,X)','The displayed set in the scan is not closed under multiplication; the corrected semidirect representation is checked algebraically.')

T(26,'1965-02-15','D. A. Kazhdan','P000047-P000052','Primary ideals and differential conditions',r'The algebraic discussion extracts first-order conditions from a square-zero thickening, then considers derivations, localization and formal flow. The notebook itself warns that I and J were interchanged. The correct prime/primary inclusions are indispensable.')
I(26,1,'P000048-P000050','method','Primary ideals through differential conditions',r'A primary ideal and its prime radical are compared, with a square inclusion and derivations vanishing on the smaller ideal; I and J are later explicitly interchanged.',r'Let \(A=k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), \(\operatorname{char}k=0\), \(P\) prime and \(Q\) \(P\)-primary with \(P^2\subset Q\subset P\). Over \(K=\operatorname{Frac}(A/P)\), linear functionals on \(P A_P/P^2A_P\) annihilating \(QA_P/P^2A_P\), extended as first-order operators, detect membership in \(Q\) together with evaluation modulo \(P\). General primary ideals require higher-order operators.','B',r'The established Noetherian-operator theory is made algebraically and algorithmically explicit by \citet[Theorem~2.1 and Sections~4--6]{cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary}, preprint January 2020, journal 2021. Its introduction traces Palamodov’s correction of constant-coefficient claims.','the method is established; constant-coefficient operators do not suffice for every primary ideal.', ['cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary'],reconstruction=r'Prime/primary letters, generic localization, polynomial ring, characteristic zero and operator values are supplied.',match='partial')
I(26,2,'P000049','construction','Conormal module and derivations',r'Derivations are used as linear functionals on an ideal modulo its square.',r'For a \(k\)-algebra \(A\), ideal \(J\), and \(A/J\)-module \(M\), any \(k\)-derivation \(D:A\to M\) kills \(J^2\) and restricts to an \(A/J\)-linear map \(J/J^2\to M\). Extension of every such functional requires additional smoothness or splitting assumptions.','E',r'This is standard conormal/differential algebra, used in the modern algebraic framework of \citet[Section~5]{cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary}.','the restriction statement is valid; surjectivity of the extension map is not automatic for an arbitrary quotient.', ['cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary'],match='partial',reconstruction=r'The module structure and the direction of the assertion are made explicit.')
I(26,3,'P000049-P000050','construction','Lifting across a square-zero ideal',r'A quotient-field embedding is lifted; a square-root equation is corrected by an element of the ideal.',r'If \(J^2=0\) in a commutative ring and \(x_0^2-y\in J\), with \(2x_0\) a unit, then \(x=x_0-(2x_0)^{-1}(x_0^2-y)\) satisfies \(x^2=y\). Lifting an entire residue field uses separability/formal smoothness, not merely that the quotient is a field.','E','The displayed square-zero correction is elementary Hensel-style algebra; the general coefficient-field statement needs the extra hypotheses made explicit here.','the corrected lifting formula holds; a completely arbitrary quotient need not split.',reconstruction=r'Square-zero, invertibility and separability conditions are reconstructed.',match='partial',verification='indirect')
I(26,4,'P000050','method','Polynomial derivations, localization and formal flow',r'Operators are written as sums \(P_i\partial_i\), extended to fractions and associated with a formal motion.',r'A \(k\)-derivation of \(k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\) is \(\sum D(x_i)\partial_i\); it extends uniquely to a localization by \(D(a/s)=(sD(a)-aD(s))/s^2\). In characteristic zero, \(\exp(tD)\) is an algebra map to \(A[[t]]\); analytic convergence is a separate issue.','E',r'This is classical differential algebra; the distinction between derivations and higher-order differential operators is explicit in \citet[Section~5]{cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary}.','these formal identities hold; the notebook formula does not describe every differential operator.', ['cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary'],reconstruction=r'Derivation rather than arbitrary differential operator, and formal rather than convergent flow, are supplied.',match='partial')
F(26,5,'P000050','remark','The solution should be an ideal',r'The discussion concludes that a solution should form an ideal.',r'the space of solutions, ring action and equations are not specified. Kernels of arbitrary differential operators are not ideals.','Primary-ideal/Noetherian-operator sources clarify a possible intended compatibility condition but do not identify this isolated sentence.')
C('P000048-P000050','prime and primary inclusions','I primary, I contains J prime, J contains I squared','Use P prime and Q P-primary with P squared contained in Q contained in P','P000050 explicitly says I and J were interchanged; scans confirm the inconsistency.',typ='ambiguity')
C('P000050','differential-operator formula','all operators sum P_i partial_i','all derivations sum P_i partial_i; general differential operators include higher derivatives','The surrounding Leibniz rule and localization distinguish derivations from general operators.',typ='ambiguity')

T(27,'1965-02-15','V. P. Palamodov','P000050-P000053','Holomorphic quotients and differential jets',r'Palamodov represents a primary-ideal quotient by differential data on its zero variety, with locally realizable compatibility. An appended growth estimate is too incomplete to recover its weighted function space.')
I(27,1,'P000051','theorem','Primary-ideal quotient by compatible differential jets',r'For a polynomial primary ideal \(J\), the quotient \(\mathcal H/J\mathcal H\) is represented by restrictions \(d(z,D)F|_N\), with local realizability.',r'For a coherent ideal \(\mathcal J\) on a Stein domain \(\Omega\), a faithful differential-jet realization of \(\mathcal O_\Omega/\mathcal J\), when chosen, has kernel \(\mathcal J\); compatible locally realizable data globalize by Cartan B. For polynomial primary ideals, suitable Noetherian operators provide such finite-order descriptions with the necessary localization and compatibility conditions.','A',r'\citet[Part~I]{palamodov1970operators} is the speaker’s subsequent systematic treatment (English 1970). \citet[Theorems~1--2]{palamodov1961ideals} is a prior smooth-function analogue, not the identical holomorphic theorem. The exact section and first publication of this formulation were not verified.',r'the Noetherian-operator and coherent-quotient framework is established, but arbitrary unrestricted vector data on \(N\) are not the target.', ['palamodov1970operators','palamodov1961ideals'],match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'Steinness, coherent quotient, operator choice and local compatibility are supplied; the 1961 smooth theorem is deliberately kept separate.')
F(27,2,'P000051','claim','Unspecified growth-controlled division estimate',r'An inequality involving the size or growth of the representing function is mentioned after the quotient theorem.',r'the norm, growth weight, domain geometry and constants are absent.','Palamodov’s 1966 An analytic problem arising in statistics (Doklady 168, 1251--1253; MathNet dan32367 opened) proves a weighted holomorphic division theorem on polydiscs. This is a related outcome, not a verified match to the missing estimate.')

T(28,'1965-02-15','A. S. Dynin','P000051-P000056','Elliptic systems, solution sheaves and finiteness',r'Local differential operators and symbols lead to solution sheaves and finite cohomology, with fine resolutions and compact functional-analytic comparison. The strongest finiteness assertion needs compatibility hypotheses omitted from the note.')
I(28,1,'P000052','theorem','Local linear maps are differential operators',r'A morphism of sheaves of smooth bundle sections is said to be differential.',r'A linear morphism of sheaves of smooth sections of finite-rank bundles on a smooth manifold is locally a finite-order differential operator (Peetre’s theorem). The order need not be globally bounded on a noncompact base.','E',r'\citet[pp.~211--218]{peetre1959differential}, with \citet[pp.~116--120]{peetre1960correction}, predates the seminar.','the local finite-order assertion is established; linearity and the sheaf locality condition are necessary.', ['peetre1959differential','peetre1960correction'],verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'Linearity, finite-rank smooth bundles and local order are explicit.')
I(28,2,'P000052-P000053','definition','Principal symbol and overdetermined ellipticity',r'The principal matrix symbol is placed over a tangent bundle and called elliptic when a monomorphism.',r'For an order-\(m\) operator \(A:C^\infty(E)\to C^\infty(F)\), \(\sigma_m(A)(x,\xi):E_x\to F_x\) is homogeneous in \(\xi\in T_x^*X\). Injectivity for real \(\xi\ne0\) is overdetermined ellipticity; an isomorphism is determined ellipticity.','E',r'\citet[p.~423]{atiyahsinger1963index} explicitly places the symbol over the cotangent sphere and defines the isomorphism case.','the modern distinction is standard; tangent and cotangent require a chosen metric to identify.', ['atiyahsinger1963index'],reconstruction=r'Cotangent bundle and real nonzero covectors supplied.',literal='false as written',match='partial')
F(28,3,'P000053','claim','Finiteness of every solution-sheaf cohomology group',r'For \(\ker A\) on a compact manifold, all cohomology groups are stated to be finite-dimensional.',r'the stated injective-symbol condition alone does not specify the exact compatibility resolution used in the proof. A sufficient precise theorem assumes a finite elliptic differential complex resolving the solution sheaf; then compactness yields finite-dimensional cohomology.','Elliptic-complex and Cartan finiteness sources identify the intended method but not a theorem in the full unstated generality of this sentence.')
I(28,4,'P000054','method','Two-term fine resolution in the locally solvable case',r'\(0\to\ker A\to\mathcal E(E_0)\to\mathcal E(E_1)\) is treated as a fine resolution.',r'If \(A\) is locally surjective as a map of smooth-section sheaves, then \(0\to\ker A\to\mathcal E(E_0)\xrightarrow A\mathcal E(E_1)\to0\) is exact. Therefore \(H^0=\ker A(X)\), \(H^1=\operatorname{coker}A(X)\), and \(H^q=0\) for \(q\ge2\). For a determined elliptic operator on compact boundaryless \(X\), the two displayed global spaces are finite-dimensional.','E',r'The resolution principle is \citet[Section~1, Proposition~1]{cartan1954finitude}; elliptic kernel/cokernel finiteness appears in \citet[p.~423]{atiyahsinger1963index}.','the conclusion is valid with local exactness; omitting it makes the resolution argument incomplete.', ['cartan1954finitude','atiyahsinger1963index'],reconstruction=r'The terminal zero, local surjectivity and no-boundary condition are supplied.')
I(28,5,'P000053-P000054','theorem','De Rham and coherent-sheaf finiteness',r'De Rham forms, holomorphic functions, Kodaira and Cartan are examples of the sheaf approach.',r'The de Rham complex is a fine resolution of the constant real sheaf. On a compact complex manifold every coherent analytic sheaf has finite-dimensional cohomology. These are separate statements; the holomorphic structure sheaf is not fine.','E',r'\citet[p.~17-1 and Section~1]{cartan1954finitude}, lecture 3 May 1954, states coherent finiteness and the de Rham example; its reference~1 is Cartan--Serre’s 1953 announcement.','both examples are classical; they require their respective smooth or analytic hypotheses.', ['cartan1954finitude'],reconstruction=r'Coherence and the distinct resolutions are explicit.')
I(28,6,'P000054-P000055','method','Compact comparison on resolutions',r'Banach/Fréchet sheaves, different resolutions and a compact comparison inducing identity on cohomology are proposed.',r'If two resolutions have continuous chain maps inducing inverse maps on cohomology, and the composite induces a compact identity on a Hausdorff Banach cohomology space, that space is finite-dimensional. Closed ranges and quotient topologies are necessary in this formulation.','E',r'The analytic finiteness method is exemplified by \citet[proof of the finiteness theorem]{cartan1954finitude}; the source invokes a Schwartz compactness lemma without its precise functional-analytic hypotheses.','the compact-identity argument works in the reconstructed setting; an arbitrary non-Hausdorff quotient does not qualify.', ['cartan1954finitude'],reconstruction=r'Hausdorff Banach cohomology, continuity and closed-range requirements are supplied.',match='partial')
C('P000052','symbol base','T(X), tangent bundle','T*(X), cotangent bundle, unless a metric identification is chosen','The scan says tangent; symbol variables are covectors. Atiyah--Singer 1963 p423 confirms the standard base.')

T(29,'1965-02-15','I. M. Gelfand','P000054-P000056','Fredholm sheaf questions and broader ellipticity',r'Gelfand reduces one example to a point and asks about compact perturbations of sheaf maps, infinite-order ellipticity and low-regularity hypoellipticity. Apart from the point case, the operator categories are not specified sufficiently to create definite open problems.')
I(29,1,'P000055','theorem','Identity plus compact operator at a point',r'For a one-point space the sheaf question becomes identity plus a compact operator.',r'For a Banach space \(X\) and compact linear \(K:X\to X\), \(I+K\) is Fredholm with index zero.','E',r'This is classical Riesz--Fredholm theory, contained in the compact-perturbation framework recalled by \citet[p.~861]{smale1965sard}.','the point case is established, including closed range and finite-dimensional kernel/cokernel.', ['smale1965sard'],reconstruction=r'Banach topology and linear compactness supplied.')
F(29,2,'P000055','question','Finite index for a topological sheaf endomorphism',r'An identity-plus-compact sheaf analogue is asked for.',r'topologies on sections, meaning of compactness, the cohomology topology and the required index are absent.','Cartan finiteness and Schwartz compact-perturbation sources provide possible frameworks, but none uniquely matches the sentence.')
F(29,3,'P000055','question','Infinite-order elliptic operators',r'Ellipticity is suggested for infinite-order equations.',r'no symbol class, growth, function space or notion of ellipticity is given.','Palamodov and pseudodifferential/infinite-order ellipticity searches return many incompatible categories. No precise unaddressed problem follows.')
F(29,4,'P000055','question','Hypoellipticity with continuous coefficients',r'A question mentions hypoellipticity and continuous coefficients.',r'operator order, domains, weak-solution meaning and target regularity are not fixed; ordinary \(C^\infty\)-hypoellipticity is not defined by the phrase alone.','Low-regularity elliptic/hypoelliptic searches did not identify a specific conjecture corresponding to this fragment.')

T(30,'1965-02-22','S. P. Novikov','P000054-P000060','Adams’s vector-fields theorem and real K-theory',r'Novikov explains the vector-fields-on-spheres theorem through elementary constructions, Bott periodicity, real projective K-theory and stunted projective spaces. The Adams theorem was published in 1962. A factor 6 in the periodicity sentence and an order statement are faithful notebook errors.')
I(30,1,'P000056','theorem','Elementary sphere obstructions and examples',r'Even spheres have no nonzero field; odd spheres have one; \(S^3,S^7\) are parallelizable.',r'For even \(n>0\), \(\chi(S^n)=2\) obstructs a nowhere-zero tangent field. On \(S^{2m-1}\subset\mathbb C^m\), \(x\mapsto ix\) is tangent and nonzero. Quaternionic and octonionic multiplication give frames on \(S^3,S^7\).','E',r'These classical examples precede the upper-bound theorem of \citet[Section~1]{adams1962vector}.',r'the assertions hold; \(S^1\) is also parallelizable.', ['adams1962vector'],reconstruction=r'The explicit complex multiplication and \(n>0\) convention are supplied.')
I(30,2,'P000056-P000057','construction','Block extension of tangent fields',r'An existing field is applied separately to coordinate blocks, multiplied by their norms.',r'From continuous independent tangent fields \(v_1,\ldots,v_k\) on \(S^{m-1}\), define on \(S^{pm-1}\) the block field \(w_i(y_1,\ldots,y_p)=(|y_j|v_i(y_j/|y_j|))_j\), with zero at a zero block. These are continuous, tangent and independent; smooth approximation preserves independence.','E',r'This elementary construction supplies lower-bound intuition in the setting of \citet[Section~1]{adams1962vector}. Its formula is directly checkable from the notebook.','the extension works; differentiability at a zero block is not automatic for the raw formula.', ['adams1962vector'],reconstruction=r'Zero-block convention and the continuity/smoothing distinction are supplied.',match='partial')
I(30,3,'P000057','theorem','Hurwitz--Radon--Adams number',r'Write \(m=2^b(2a+1)\), \(b=c+4d\), \(0\leq c\leq3\); the maximum number is \(2^c+8d-1\).',r'If \(m=(2a+1)2^{4d+c}\), then \(S^{m-1}\) has exactly \(\rho(m)-1\) independent continuous tangent fields, where \(\rho(m)=8d+2^c\).','E',r'\citet[Theorem~1.1]{adams1962vector}, received November 1961 and published 1962, proves the matching upper bound to the classical lower construction.',r'the number is exact in every dimension. Multiplication of \(m\) by 16, not 6, increases it by 8.', ['adams1962vector'],literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The main boxed formula is correct; a later prose periodicity sentence is corrected separately.')
I(30,4,'P000057-P000058','definition','Stable vector bundles and KO',r'Stable equivalence, direct sums, tensor products and the real K-functor are described.',r'For compact \(X\), \(KO^0(X)\) is the Grothendieck group of real vector bundles, with tensor-product multiplication. For connected based \(X\), \(\widetilde{KO}^0(X)\) is the rank-zero subgroup. Stable equivalence of actual bundles alone forgets rank and must be distinguished from full \(KO^0\).','E',r'This is the K-theoretic language of \citet[Sections~1--2]{adams1962vector}; the index-theory use appears already in \citet[p.~423]{atiyahsinger1963index} for complex K-theory.','the unreduced/reduced distinction resolves the notebook’s missing rank summand.', ['adams1962vector','atiyahsinger1963index'],reconstruction=r'Group completion and reduced group are distinguished.')
I(30,5,'P000057-P000058','theorem','Bott periodicity and stable groups',r'The stable orthogonal groups recur with period eight and nontrivial entries at residues 0,1,2,4.',r'\(KO^{-j}(\mathrm{pt})\), for \(j=0,\ldots,7\), is respectively \(\mathbb Z,\mathbb Z/2,\mathbb Z/2,0,\mathbb Z,0,0,0\), repeating with period eight. For \(i>0\), \(\widetilde{KO}^0(S^i)=\pi_i(BO)\).','E',r'\citet[Introduction, stable classical-group periodicity]{bott1959stable}, received November 1958 and published September 1959, is the primary source.','periodicity is established; the sphere formula requires reduced K-theory.', ['bott1959stable'],reconstruction=r'The table is in KO-degree conventions, not unshifted \(\pi_i(O)\).',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
I(30,6,'P000057-P000059','theorem','Real K-theory of projective space',r'The reduced group is cyclic; the tautological class satisfies \(v^2=1\), \(\lambda=v-1\), \(\lambda^2=-2\lambda\). The order is once described as \(f(k)\).',r'For \(k\ge1\), let \(\phi(k)\) count \(1\le j\le k\) congruent to \(0,1,2,4\pmod8\). Then \(KO^0(\mathbb{RP}^k)=\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z/2^{\phi(k)}\), with reduced generator \(\lambda=[\gamma]-1\), satisfying \(\lambda^2=-2\lambda\).','E',r'The real-projective computation is part of \citet[projective-space K-theory calculation]{adams1962vector}.',r'the correct order is \(2^{\phi(k)}\), in agreement with another line of the same notebook.', ['adams1962vector'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The tautological bundle is reduced by subtracting the trivial line; the exponent is defined explicitly.')
I(30,7,'P000059','construction','Reflection map and stunted projective space',r'An axis determines an orthogonal reflection; a stunted projective quotient maps into a Stiefel quotient.',r'The map \([v]\mapsto I-2vv^t\) from \(\mathbb{RP}^{n-1}\) to \(O(n)\), for unit \(v\), induces \(\mathbb{RP}^{n-1}/\mathbb{RP}^{n-k-2}\to O(n)/O(n-k-1)\) by taking the last \(k+1\) columns. The collapsed subspace is precisely where those coordinates of \(v\) vanish.','E',r'Stunted projective spaces are central to \citet[Theorem~1.2 and ensuing reduction]{adams1962vector}. The explicit reflection formula is editorial clarification of the drawing.','the construction is well-defined; indexing must match the chosen Stiefel frame size.', ['adams1962vector'],reconstruction=r'A specific block convention for the Stiefel quotient is supplied.',match='partial')
C('P000057','periodicity sentence','m increases by factor 6, fields increase by 8','m increases by factor 16, fields increase by 8','Scan shows 6; the displayed m=2^(c+4d)(2a+1) and d+1 imply 16.')
C('P000058','K-theory of sphere','K_R(S^i)=pi_i(BO)','Reduced KO of S^i equals pi_i(BO) for i>0','Rank summand omitted in the scan; the surrounding stable-bundle discussion supports reduced interpretation.',typ='ambiguity')
C('P000059','cyclic group order','cyclic group of order f(k)','cyclic group of order 2^f(k)','The scan states order f(k) but later says add the generator 2^f(k) times; the latter matches Adams.')

T(31,'1965-03-01','V. P. Palamodov','P000058-P000061','Primary-ideal quotient revisited',r'P000060 restates the compatible differential-jet realization of a primary polynomial ideal quotient and says Kazhdan obtains it algebraically. It is a recurrence of record 27, not a separate fully specified growth theorem.')
I(31,1,'P000060','theorem','Noetherian differential jets and the algebraic proof',r'\(\mathcal H/J\mathcal H\cong\mathcal H\{J\}\), with \(\mathcal H\{J\}\) locally realized by \(d(z,D)F|_N\); Kazhdan is said to obtain this algebraically.',r'The quotient is represented by compatible finite differential jets on the primary support, once suitable Noetherian operators and the locally realizable target sheaf are fixed. Algebraically, a primary ideal in characteristic-zero polynomial rings is the simultaneous vanishing modulo its radical of finitely many polynomial-coefficient differential operators.','A',r'The speaker’s systematic treatment is \citet[Part~I]{palamodov1970operators}; the algebraic operator statement is explicitly formulated in \citet[Theorem~2.1 and equation~(17)]{cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary}. The latter circulated January 2020, published 2021.','the framework is established; the note does not identify a separate Kazhdan publication or supply all analytic globalization hypotheses.', ['palamodov1970operators','cidruizhomssturmfels2021primary'],reconstruction=r'The operator construction and target compatibility are supplied; no attribution of the modern algorithm to Kazhdan is made.',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium')
