T(32,'1965-03-01','I. M. Gelfand','P000059-P000062','Nelson’s commuting differential operators',r'P000060--P000061 distinguishes essential self-adjointness from formal symmetry and strong commutation from commutation on a core. The punctured genus-four surface is Nelson’s example, already published in 1959.')
I(32,1,'P000060','theorem','Essential self-adjointness and boundary conditions',r'The range of \(A-\lambda\) is required to be dense for every complex \(\lambda\); differentiation on an interval is compared with periodic boundary conditions.',r'For a densely defined symmetric \(A\), essential self-adjointness is equivalent to density of both \(\operatorname{ran}(A-i)\) and \(\operatorname{ran}(A+i)\). On \(L^2(0,1)\), \(i\,d/dx\) on \(H^1_0(0,1)\) has deficiency indices \((1,1)\); the periodic \(H^1\) domain gives a self-adjoint extension.','E',r'These are the operator-theoretic preliminaries to \citet[Sections~9--10]{nelson1959analytic}. The range criterion needs a pair of nonreal parameters, not all complex numbers.','the distinction between a symmetric operator, its closure and a chosen self-adjoint extension is essential.', ['nelson1959analytic'],literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'Dense domain, the nonreal parameters, and the precise Sobolev domains are supplied.',match='partial')
I(32,2,'P000060-P000061','counterexample','Commuting on a domain does not imply strong commutation',r'\(A,B\) commute on an invariant dense domain, and all real linear combinations are essentially self-adjoint, yet their exponentials need not commute.',r'There exist symmetric \(A,B\) on a common invariant dense domain \(D\), with \(ABf=BAf\) for \(f\in D\), such that every \(aA+bB\), \(a,b\in\mathbb R\), is essentially self-adjoint on \(D\), while the spectral measures of their closures do not commute.','E',r'\citet[Section~10]{nelson1959analytic}, published 1959, constructs precisely this obstruction. Essential self-adjointness of a sum of squares is a stronger sufficient integrability condition.','the counterexample remains valid; separate essential self-adjointness does not license joint functional calculus.', ['nelson1959analytic'],literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The distinction between algebraic commutation and commuting spectral measures is explicit.')
I(32,3,'P000061','construction','The punctured genus-four translation surface',r'A planar region with an inner square removed is glued by translations; after identification of corner points, it is a genus-four surface minus one point.',r'On Nelson’s translation surface, \(X=\partial_x,Y=\partial_y\) commute locally on \(C_c^\infty\). Almost-everywhere complete straight-line flows generate unitary groups on area \(L^2\); \(iX,iY\), and every real linear combination, are essentially self-adjoint on this domain. The two global flows fail to commute across the missing point.','E',r'\citet[Section~10; Russian translation pp.~121--123]{nelson1959analytic} explicitly gives genus four. The notebook drawing agrees; replacing four by two would introduce an error.','the geometric example is established and the source genus is retained.', ['nelson1959analytic'],literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The invariant area measure, compactly supported smooth domain and factors of \(i\) are supplied.')
I(32,4,'P000061','theorem','The Laplacian obstruction to integrating the Lie algebra',r'If the Laplacian were self-adjoint, all fields would generate the expected group; therefore the example’s Laplacian is not essentially self-adjoint.',r'For a representation of a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra by skew-symmetric operators \(X_j\) on a common invariant dense domain, essential self-adjointness of the Nelson Laplacian \(-\sum_jX_j^2\) is a sufficient condition for integration to a unitary representation of the simply connected group. Thus this sufficient condition fails for Nelson’s noncommuting translation example.','E',r'\citet[Section~9, integrability theorem, and Section~10]{nelson1959analytic} proves the criterion and applies it to the example.','the conclusion concerns essential self-adjointness on the specified domain; it does not deny the existence of self-adjoint extensions.', ['nelson1959analytic'],reconstruction=r'A common invariant domain, the simply connected integrating group and the sign convention are supplied.')
I(32,5,'P000061','method','Spectral construction of the unitary evolution',r'The exponent of a self-adjoint operator is written using an unnormalized resolvent integral, and the differential equation \(u_t=iAu\) is invoked.',r'For self-adjoint \(A\), \(e^{itA}=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{it\lambda}\,dE_A(\lambda)\). This is a strongly continuous unitary group on all \(H\); strong differentiation solves \(u_t=iAu\) for initial vectors in \(D(A)\). Resolvent boundary values recover \(E_A\) through Stone’s jump formula, not through the unqualified integral in the note.','E',r'The spectral and unitary-group framework is used throughout \citet[Sections~9--10]{nelson1959analytic}; the displayed correction follows directly from spectral calculus.','the unitary evolution is established, with a domain condition for strong differentiability.', ['nelson1959analytic'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'Spectral measure, normalization and differentiability domain are supplied.')
C('P000060','dense-range criterion','the image of A-lambda is dense for all complex lambda','density for A-i and A+i (equivalently the appropriate nonreal parameters)','The scan includes all complex parameters; real eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator contradict that wording.')
C('P000061','resolvent formula for evolution','integral exp(it lambda)/(iA-lambda) dlambda','spectral integral exp(it lambda) dE_A(lambda), or a specified normalized resolvent-jump formula','The scan omits contour/boundary prescription and normalization; spectral calculus gives the precise replacement.')

T(33,'1965-03-01','Ya. G. Sinai','P000060-P000064','Simple eigenvalues and countable Lebesgue spectrum',r'P000062 and the top of P000063 distinguish simple eigenspaces from the continuous spectral multiplicity of toral automorphisms and Bernoulli shifts. The remark that all known Lebesgue examples have countable multiplicity is historical; no explicit Banach problem is posed.')
I(33,1,'P000062','theorem','Simplicity of eigenvalues for an ergodic system',r'For an ergodic system every eigenvalue is simple; invariant functions are constant.',r'For an ergodic probability-preserving \(\mathbb Z\)-action or \(\mathbb R\)-flow, each Koopman eigenspace is one-dimensional. A nonzero eigenfunction has constant modulus, and the ratio of two eigenfunctions for the same character is invariant. This does not assert simple continuous spectrum.','E',r'The classical pure-point classification is restated precisely in \citet[Introduction and Theorem~5.5]{hermlekreidler2023halmos}; its antecedent is Halmos--von Neumann (1942).','the statement is correct under ergodicity; the source heading concerns discrete spectrum.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Probability preservation and the distinction between eigenspaces and continuous multiplicity are supplied.',match='partial')
I(33,2,'P000062','definition','Absolute continuity versus homogeneous Lebesgue spectrum',r'Correlation functions are Fourier transforms of integrable densities, and a constant spectral multiplicity is called homogeneous.',r'For the centered Koopman space \(L^2_0(X,\mu)\), absolute continuity means every scalar spectral measure is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure on \(\mathbb R\) for flows or Haar measure on \(\mathbb T\) for transformations. Lebesgue maximal type additionally means equivalence of measure classes; homogeneous multiplicity means the multiplicity function is almost everywhere constant.','E',r'\citet[Introduction, pp.~899--900]{sinai1961lebesgue} distinguishes the homogeneous Lebesgue conclusion and proves countable multiplicity in its class of flows.','absolute continuity alone does not guarantee full Lebesgue maximal type or homogeneous multiplicity; constants must be removed.', ['sinai1961lebesgue'],reconstruction=r'Centered space, measure-class equivalence and the time-group distinction are explicit.',match='partial')
I(33,3,'P000062','remark','Historical multiplicity remark and an editorial Banach comparison',r'All known examples of systems with Lebesgue spectrum are said to have countably infinite multiplicity. There is no explicit existence question, no Banach attribution, and no fixed choice between transformations and flows.',r'The exact source claim is historical. An editorial comparison asks whether an ergodic probability-preserving transformation or flow can have simple Lebesgue spectrum on \(L^2_0\); these are different questions. The notebook does not uniquely specify either as a proposed problem.','F',r'For flows, \citet[main result]{prikhodko2020flows} gives a positive construction; the paper was received in June 2012, revised October 2019, and published 2020. For transformations, \citet[Introduction, main theorem]{openai2026lebesgue}, dated 23 September 2026, claims a smooth volume-preserving three-torus diffeomorphism with a bilateral Koopman orbit forming an orthonormal basis of \(L^2_0\). Its theorem statement was inspected, but its proof was not independently verified here.','simple Lebesgue flows have a published construction; a new transformation claim exists, and this audit does not establish an accepted resolution of the transformation problem. Neither modern question is assigned to the notebook as an explicit conjecture.', ['prikhodko2020flows','openai2026lebesgue'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'The modern comparison is editorial, the flow/transformation distinction is supplied, and the 2026 claim is separated from verified established results.')
I(33,4,'P000062','example','Hyperbolic toral automorphisms have countable Lebesgue spectrum',r'The integer-matrix automorphism of the two-torus is diagonalized by Fourier modes whose integer labels run in infinite orbits.',r'For \(A\in SL(2,\mathbb Z)\) with \(|\operatorname{tr}A|>2\), the automorphism \(x\mapsto Ax\) of the Haar probability torus has on \(L^2_0\) homogeneous countably infinite Lebesgue spectrum: each orbit of \(A^t\) on \(\mathbb Z^2\setminus\{0\}\) gives a bilateral shift.','E',r'\citet[p.~899]{sinai1961lebesgue} identifies compact abelian-group automorphisms among the already known algebraic examples. The Fourier-orbit argument is explicit in the notebook.',r'the result holds under hyperbolicity; an arbitrary matrix in \(SL(2,\mathbb Z)\) need not satisfy it.', ['sinai1961lebesgue'],reconstruction=r'Haar probability, removal of constants and hyperbolicity are supplied.',match='partial')
I(33,5,'P000062-P000063','example','Bernoulli shifts have countable Lebesgue spectrum',r'A tensor product of independent coordinates is organized into translation orbits.',r'A nontrivial two-sided Bernoulli shift on a standard probability product has homogeneous countably infinite Lebesgue spectrum on \(L^2_0\). Finite tensor words in centered coordinate basis vectors form an orthonormal basis; their shift orbits are free and countably many.','E',r'This is a classical example within the discrete-time Kolmogorov setting recalled in \citet[Introduction, pp.~899--900]{sinai1961lebesgue}. The tensor-basis proof is directly reconstructed from P000062--P000063.','the statement holds for the standard probability and separability setting; the constant tensor is excluded.', ['sinai1961lebesgue'],reconstruction=r'Nontrivial base, two-sided shift, separability and centered space supplied.',match='partial')
I(33,6,'P000063','method','Spectral measures of tensor products are convolutions',r'Product eigenfunctions produce sums of frequencies, and the associated measure is called a product measure.',r'For \(\mathbb R\)-representations, the scalar spectral measure of \(v\otimes w\) under \(U_t\otimes V_t\) is the pushforward of \(\mu_v\otimes\nu_w\) by \((\lambda,\eta)\mapsto\lambda+\eta\), hence convolution. For \(\mathbb Z\), use multiplication on \(\mathbb T\).','E',r'The product-representation setting already appears in \citet[Section~3]{bruhat1954induced}, a December 1952 lecture printed in 1954. The pushforward follows by multiplying the two correlation Fourier transforms.','the tensor-product formula is established; the measure on the one-dimensional dual is a pushforward, not the raw Cartesian product.', ['bruhat1954induced'],reconstruction=r'The acting group is the diagonal subgroup and the spectral pushforward is explicit.',match='partial')
C('P000062','Lebesgue spectrum convention','densities for all f in L2','use L2_0 for a probability-preserving system and distinguish absolute continuity from equivalent Lebesgue maximal type','The constant function has an atom at the trivial character; the scan does not exclude it.',typ='ambiguity')
C('P000063','tensor spectral measure','product measure on the spectral line','pushforward of the product measure under addition, or multiplication on the circle','The preceding addition of frequencies in the scan determines convolution.',typ='ambiguity')
