T(34,'1965-03-22','A. A. Kirillov','P000062-P000064','Group actions, spectral reconstruction and cocycles',r'The actual record runs from P000063 through P000065, beyond the catalogue envelope. It compares compact homogeneous actions, noncompact representations, cocycles and reconstruction. The finite-group reconstruction question has negative examples; several cohomological remarks remain too indefinite to identify.')
I(34,1,'P000063','definition','Koopman spectra for general group actions',r'Dynamical systems are extended to arbitrary groups, with irreducible representations replacing scalar eigenvalues.',r'A measurable probability-preserving action of a locally compact second-countable group \(G\) on a standard probability space gives a unitary Koopman representation. For a type-I group the usual essentially unique irreducible direct-integral decomposition permits a spectral measure-class and multiplicity description. Arbitrary groups require more care; such data are not automatically a complete classification.','E',r'The distinction between a representation and an action is central to \citet[Introduction and Section~6]{hermlekreidler2023halmos}. \citet[Section~2]{gelfandfomin1952geodesic} already uses irreducible decomposition for the relevant semisimple groups.','the representation-theoretic construction is established; the unrestricted classification language needs the type-I and reconstruction qualifications.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos','gelfandfomin1952geodesic'],reconstruction=r'Standard measure spaces, continuity and the type-I restriction on the familiar spectral parametrization are supplied.',match='partial')
I(34,2,'P000063','theorem','Ergodic compact-group actions are essentially homogeneous',r'For a compact group, an ergodic action is described by a homogeneous space.',r'An ergodic probability-preserving action of a second-countable compact group on a standard probability space is, modulo null sets, its action on \(G/H\) for a closed subgroup \(H\), with invariant quotient Haar probability. Equivariant isomorphism corresponds to conjugacy of stabilizers.','E',r'The homogeneous model is the compact instance of \citet[Remark~6.44(iii)]{hermlekreidler2023halmos}; the stabilizer classification is explicit in \citet[Sections~2--3]{mackey1966virtual}, reviewing the earlier elementary transitive theory.','the theorem holds in the measurable standard-space formulation; pointwise transitivity of an arbitrary representative is stronger than required.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos','mackey1966virtual'],reconstruction=r'Closed stabilizer, quotient Haar measure and the modulo-null-sets conclusion are supplied.')
F(34,3,'P000063','remark','A finite-support binary group and an uncertain attribution',r'The notation is a product of copies of \(\mathbb Z/2\), but the elements are said to have finitely many nonzero coordinates; names resembling Gordin and Whitehead appear.',r'the group described is \(\bigoplus_{\mathbb N}\mathbb Z/2\), while its topology, action and the attributed result are not specified.','Searches combining the names, binary groups, spectra and ergodic actions did not identify an exact theorem; the finite-support/product discrepancy is recorded separately.')
I(34,4,'P000063-P000064','example','Dyadic roots and their compact dual',r'The dyadic roots of unity are a countable group whose characters form a compact group.',r'The discrete Prüfer group \(C_{2^\infty}=\mathbb Z[1/2]/\mathbb Z\) is the direct limit of \(C_{2^n}\). Its Pontryagin dual is the inverse limit \(\varprojlim\mathbb Z/2^n\mathbb Z=\mathbb Z_2\), with the compact inverse-limit topology.','E','The identification is classical and follows directly by restricting a character to each cyclic subgroup: the resulting residues are compatible modulo successive powers of two. No new spectral theorem is asserted.','the group and dual are unambiguous once the discrete topology is specified.',reconstruction=r'Direct-limit and inverse-limit maps are supplied.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='none found',verification='indirect',confidence='medium')
F(34,5,'P000064','remark','Unspecified dyadic spectral decomposition',r'A remark about point and continuous spectra and pieces of functions follows the dyadic group example.',r'no action, invariant measure or definite decomposition assertion is supplied.','The dyadic-group identification does not determine the spectrum of an unspecified action; no unique publication match was found.')
I(34,6,'P000064','theorem','The full real affine group has one infinite-dimensional irreducible',r'The group \(x\mapsto ax+b\), \(a\ne0\), is said to have one infinite-dimensional irreducible representation, besides characters.',r'For \(G=\mathbb R\rtimes\mathbb R^\times\), the one-dimensional unitary characters are \((b,a)\mapsto |a|^{it}\operatorname{sgn}(a)^\varepsilon\), \(t\in\mathbb R,\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}\), and there is exactly one infinite-dimensional irreducible unitary representation up to equivalence. Restricting the group to \(a>0\) gives two such irreducibles instead.','E',r'\citet[Sections~2.2--2.3 and Section~5]{choighandehari2021affine} explicitly records this classical Mackey classification and distinguishes the connected subgroup.','the notebook’s word one is correct for the full group; replacing it by two would silently change the group.', ['choighandehari2021affine'],literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The full group, continuous unitary representations and both one-dimensional character parameters are explicit.')
I(34,7,'P000064','claim','Discrete full-group spectrum on a compact hyperbolic quotient',r'For a compact surface of negative curvature the relevant Lorentz-group representation is said to have discrete spectrum.',r'Let \(G=PSL(2,\mathbb R)\) and \(\Gamma\) be cocompact discrete. The right representation of \(G\) on \(L^2(\Gamma\backslash G)\) is a discrete Hilbert sum of irreducibles, each with finite multiplicity. Its restriction to the geodesic one-parameter subgroup has Lebesgue spectrum on \(L^2_0\), so the two uses of spectrum must not be identified.','E',r'\citet[Sections~2--3, especially p.~132]{gelfandfomin1952geodesic} establishes the continuous-time Lebesgue conclusion. For the full-group discreteness assertion, compactness makes integrated compactly supported continuous kernels compact; the usual compact-operator decomposition supplies the discrete sum.','the full-group and one-parameter assertions coexist; compactness alone does not give pure-point geodesic-flow spectrum.', ['gelfandfomin1952geodesic'],reconstruction=r'The acting group, cocompactness and the distinction between full-group decomposition and flow spectrum are supplied.',match='partial')
I(34,8,'P000064','definition','Measurable cocycles and coboundaries',r'Operators have form \(T_gf(x)=f(xg)\chi(x,g)\); equivalent multipliers differ by a ratio of a function at \(xg\) and \(x\).',r'For a right probability-preserving action, a measurable \(\mathbb T\)-valued cocycle satisfies \(\alpha(x,gh)=\alpha(x,g)\alpha(xg,h)\), almost everywhere with the chosen measurable formulation. It defines unitary \(T_gf(x)=\alpha(x,g)f(xg)\). Replacing \(\alpha\) by \(b(x)^{-1}\alpha(x,g)b(xg)\) conjugates the representation by multiplication by \(b\).','E',r'\citet[Section~5; Russian translation pp.~127--128]{mackey1966virtual} gives the cocycle and cohomology formulas and explains their 1963 announcement.','the construction is established; measurable equivalence and changes on null sets require the standard cocycle framework.', ['mackey1966virtual'],reconstruction=r'Unit-modulus values, preservation of measure and an explicit right-action convention are supplied.')
I(34,9,'P000064','theorem','Transitive cocycles and stabilizer representations',r'For a transitive action the cocycle classes are identified with representations of the subgroup.',r'For the homogeneous right action on \(H\backslash G\), strict Borel cocycles with values in a compact group \(K\), up to Borel cohomology, correspond to continuous homomorphisms \(H\to K\) up to conjugacy. With \(K=\mathbb T\) these are characters of \(H\); with \(K=U(n)\) they are \(n\)-dimensional unitary representations.','E',r'\citet[Section~5, final transitive-case paragraph]{mackey1966virtual} gives precisely the homomorphism correspondence; \citet[Section~2]{bruhat1954induced} supplies the earlier induced-representation framework.','the scalar formula yields characters, while higher-dimensional representations require vector-valued cocycles.', ['mackey1966virtual','bruhat1954induced'],reconstruction=r'Strict Borel representatives, closed stabilizer and the coefficient group are specified.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
I(34,10,'P000064','programme','Virtual subgroups and ergodic groupoids',r'Mackey’s generalized subgroups and the analogy between transitive and ergodic actions are discussed.',r'The identifiable programme replaces a homogeneous action’s stabilizer by a virtual subgroup described through an ergodic measured groupoid. It is a framework for actions, cocycles and induction, not a single unsolved conjecture.','E',r'\citet{mackey1963ergodic} announced the programme before this seminar; \citet[Introduction and Sections~9--11]{mackey1966virtual} supplies the systematic explanation and the groupoid formulation.','the programme became an established part of measured groupoid and ergodic-action theory; the source contains no uniquely delimited remaining question.', ['mackey1963ergodic','mackey1966virtual'],literal='correct',reconstruction=r'The later groupoid language identifies the programme without assigning every later result to the seminar.',match='partial')
F(34,11,'P000064-P000065','question','Does cohomology determine the dynamical system?',r'A broad reconstruction question asks whether cohomology determines a system.',r'the cohomology degree, coefficient object, additional structure to retain and equivalence of systems are unspecified.','Mackey’s cocycle programme and later reconstruction results delimit several inequivalent readings. They do not identify this fragment with a particular modern open problem.')
I(34,12,'P000065','construction','Products of eigenfunctions and a symmetric two-cocycle',r'Products of normalized eigenfunctions differ from the eigenfunction of the product eigenvalue by constants, satisfying a cocycle law.',r'For an ergodic probability-preserving abelian action, choose unit-modulus eigenfunctions \(f_\lambda\). Then \(f_\lambda f_\mu=c(\lambda,\mu)f_{\lambda\mu}\), with \(c\in\mathbb T\); associativity gives the two-cocycle identity and commutativity gives symmetry. Changing phases changes \(c\) by a coboundary. With pure-point spectrum the eigenvalue subgroup classifies the action; otherwise this describes its Kronecker factor only.','E',r'\citet[Theorem~5.5 and the preceding eigenfunction construction]{hermlekreidler2023halmos} gives a modern precise version of the 1942 Halmos--von Neumann classification. The two-cocycle equations follow directly by associating a triple product.','the source construction is correct; full reconstruction requires pure-point spectrum and the standard probability-space setting.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos'],reconstruction=r'Abelian action, normalized modulus, phase equivalence and the pure-point restriction are supplied.',match='partial')
I(34,13,'P000065','question','Unitary spectrum does not determine even a finite ergodic action',r'Whether the spectrum determines the system is said to be unclear even for finite groups; Gelfand answers that it does not.',r'The answer is negative even for transitive finite actions. Let \(G=GL(3,\mathbb F_2)\) act on the seven nonzero vectors of \(\mathbb F_2^3\), and on the seven nonzero covectors by the dual action. The permutation characters coincide, but the \(G\)-sets are not isomorphic. With uniform probability these are ergodic systems with unitarily equivalent Koopman representations.','E',r'\citet[Remark~6.44(iii)]{hermlekreidler2023halmos} records the obstruction already noted by Mackey (1964, Section~3, Remark~2), citing Todd’s 1950 example. The explicit seven-point verification here is editorial: fixed-vector and fixed-covector counts agree by rank equality, while the stabilizer of a vector fixes no nonzero covector.','the finite-group question has a negative answer; this is not an open reconstruction problem.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos'],problem='GP-1964-W04',reconstruction=r'The seven-point example is supplied as an elementary check, not attributed to the notebook or asserted to be Todd’s exact presentation.',verification='indirect',match='exact')
I(34,14,'P000065','example','The icosahedral rotation group needs a central quotient',r'The icosahedral rotation group is identified with the unimodular group over five elements.',r'The orientation-preserving icosahedral group is \(A_5\cong PSL(2,\mathbb F_5)=SL(2,\mathbb F_5)/\{\pm I\}\), of order \(60\). The group \(SL(2,\mathbb F_5)\) itself has order \(120\).','E',r'This is the classical finite-group identification. The immediate order check already forces the central quotient: \(|SL(2,\mathbb F_q)|=q(q^2-1)\), and the center at \(q=5\) has two elements.','the missing projective quotient is a faithful mathematical error in the note.',literal='false as written',reconstruction=r'The quotient by the center is supplied.',match='none found',verification='indirect',confidence='medium')
I(34,15,'P000065','principle','Multiplicative observables recover the action',r'The spectral representation forgets multiplication; a ring of functions with its group action restores the system.',r'For standard probability spaces, a state-preserving normal unital \(*\)-isomorphism between their commutative \(L^\infty\) algebras that intertwines the group actions gives an isomorphism of measure algebras, hence a spatial conjugacy modulo null sets. Retaining just the \(L^2\) representation discards this multiplicative information.','E',r'\citet[Section~1.2 and Theorem~4.8]{hermlekreidler2023halmos} makes the reconstruction category explicit through Markov lattice homomorphisms and compact models.','the structured observable algebra is sufficient in the stated category; an abstract spectrum alone is not.', ['hermlekreidler2023halmos'],reconstruction=r'The algebraic, order/normality and probability-state structure is explicit.',match='partial')
